# Jewellery counter UX

Follow this on every screen. Use `AppColors`, `AppTypography`, and Inter from `AppTheme`. Do not introduce new brand hex codes or another font.

## Identity

- Font is Inter. Body text and input content are 16sp. The sign-in title is 32sp. Other screen headings are 24–28sp. Labels are 14sp and stay legible when the system text size is scaled. The sign-in header keeps both face login and help.
- Logo stays the white-and-gold mark on a black surface. Do not recolor it.
- Work areas use warm work surface `#F4F3F7`. Actions use charcoal `#20201F`. Accents use champagne `#E6C477` and gold text `#866526`.
- Gold guides attention. Do not fill every control with gold.

## Sign-in

- Keep username and password labels above the inputs. No extra password tab and no large decorative gradient.
- Password visibility, recovery, and support stay visible.
- "Remember me" stores the username only. Never store a raw password.
- Custom API lives in Connection settings, with validation and restricted access.
- Add biometric sign-in only when the device has secure, enrolled authentication.

## Scanner and layout

- Touch targets are at least 48×48dp. The main action is 56dp high.
- Inventory and Search stay in the first view. Pair icons with text.
- Keep Sample in and Sample out visually distinct.
- Use Android system bars and back behaviour. Do not build an iPhone-style shell.
- Check the smallest handheld width, large text, and one-handed use.

## Status

- Reader ready, reader unavailable, and sync state come from the real device and backend.
- A scan is not a completed transaction. Keep scan, exception review, confirm, and sync separate.
- Signal feedback is guidance, never an exact distance. The physical item still has to be verified.
- Do not invent inventory totals, sync success, or connectivity.
